description:
Guardtime is a system engineering company, with deep experience in data-centric cybersecurity solutions and heavily engaged in R&D in hash-based cryptography. Guardtime‘s core technology - KSI® Blockchain - is designed to provide massively scalable digital signature-based authentication for electronic data, machines and humans. Guardtime has a team of 200 cryptographers, developers, and security architects, with decades of experience defending networks from nation-state attack. Guardtime‘s offices are in Estonia (Tallinn, Tartu), Netherlands (Amsterdam), Switzerland (Lausanne) and the United States (Irvine, CA and Alexandria, VA). Established in 2007 in Estonia, Guardtime‘s largest office with its core R&D division and development teams are in Tallinn (EE) and Tartu (EE). With over 50 patents filed and more pending, Guardtime has a proven track record in transforming foundational research into practical solutions. With product deployments in Europe, Middle East, Asia and the Americas, Guardtime provides data-centric cyber security solutions for verticals such as governance, space, healthcare, insurance telecommunications, energy and defence. Guardtime is also providing anti-tamper hardware for accessing the KSI® Blockchain technology stack. 
In 2019, Guardtime entered the space cyber security sector and, in close collaboration with the European Space Agency and other global partners, has been ever since been working on several issues, primarily focussing on developing space data security solutions. The past and ongoing initiatives include: 
• Earth Observation data provenance solutions related to mission control, EO long term data archives and Quality Assurance; 
• Use of distributed ledgers and blockchains in ground station and mission control to enhance security and trust. In 2021, Guardtime successfully finalised a prototype, showcasing the ways for establishing a clear consensus on Space Surveillance and Tracking (SST) data from multiple data sources, with the long term aim of establishing long term trust and secure exchange mechanisms between space operators, by deploying fully tracable information on future trajectories of space objects, on execution of manoeuvre plans to detect and prevent collisions and by enforcing rules for acknowledgement of collision risks and subsequent negotiated manouevre execution; 
• During the ESA C-SOC Phase 1, development of the concept of operations for the ESA’s first Cyber Safety and Security Operational Centre (C-SOC), to develop, integrate and validate a set of security technologies and measures focused on creating a secure environment to develop and operate ESA’s critical space systems.
